    "findingId": "F2",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "The mechanics are described accurately — `getCachedOriginal` is a `requestMemo` keyed on `src` alone (L158-166), so every caller in one request awaits one promise and receives the identical `Uint8Array`, and `getImage()`'s no-size branch passes that exact reference into its public `ResolvedImage.bytes`. What refutes the finding is that this is deliberate, test-locked behavior rather than an oversight: `imageApi.requestCache.test.ts:84-87` asserts `expect(b.bytes).toBe(a.bytes)` with the comment \"The shared entry is handed to every caller, bytes included.\" The recommended fix (`bytes: new Uint8Array(bytes)`) would break that assertion, so it is an API design change, not a bug fix. Nothing in the framework mutates the buffer: `runPipeline` hands it to `Bun.Image`, which borrows read-only (Bun's docs only warn against the *caller* mutating during a pending decode), and the debug-bar preview goes through `Buffer.from(result.bytes)`, which copies. Corruption also cannot reach disk: `FileStorage` returns binaries as lazy `BlobRef`s and `readBlobRef` materializes a fresh `Uint8Array` per read, so a mutation is confined to the current request (a same-request variant regen re-encoding from it is the only secondary effect, as the finding says, but it still requires the user to write into a buffer a framework API handed back). An implicit read-only contract on bytes returned from a cache is the near-universal convention in JS, and the authors reasoned about it explicitly in the adjacent comments (L122-123, L138-139). What remains is a legitimate documentation nit — add the caveat to `getImage()`'s JSDoc and `ResolvedImage.bytes` — not a logic bug."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F3",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "The code behaves exactly as described: both success paths call `resolveImageCacheControl(options.timeToStale, options.timeToEvict, development)` (L98, L142) with the global configured window, so a response carrying `X-Mochi-Cache: stale` also carries `max-age=14400, stale-while-revalidate=72000`. But this is the documented, tested contract, unchanged since the feature landed (`git log -S` points only at #65). `packages/docs/148-images.md` states: \"Served images carry an `ETag` and a `Cache-Control` derived from the cache window (`public, max-age=<timeToStale>, stale-while-revalidate=<timeToEvict − timeToStale>`)\" — i.e. derived from the configured window by design — and `imageEndpoint.test.ts:11-43` locks that mapping. The same page pre-empts the finding's headline impact: \"In development no `Cache-Control` is sent, so edits and `invalidateImage()` calls always show up on the next request\", which is an explicit acknowledgement that in production they intentionally do not. `X-Mochi-Cache` is origin telemetry that HTTP caches ignore, so there is no protocol contradiction — an origin is entitled to assign a freshness lifetime to the representation it just served, and emitting a fixed window per response is the standard ISR-style pattern. The recommended fix is also unsound for the case it emphasizes: `MochiCache.markStale` (cache.ts:238-256) backdates only the envelope `createdAt`, while `metaFrom` derives `staleAt`/`evictAt` from the inner generation stamp `value.createdAt`, so `meta.staleAt - Date.now()` would still yield a full window right after a soft `invalidateImage()`; the endpoint would have to branch on `status` instead (the timestamps it receives cannot express a soft invalidation at all). Deriving max-age from remaining freshness would be a genuine improvement to invalidation latency, but as written this is a design critique of documented, intentional behavior, not a defect."
  },
