    "findingId": "F4",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "The code reading is correct — `cascadeSeq` is a bare module-level `let` (L130) feeding a `setHandler` name (L182), `setHandler` does replace by name (events.ts:498-509), `mochiEvents` is `pinGlobal`'d, and `requestCache.ts:178` really does pin its analogous counter for the stated reason. The analogy nonetheless fails, and that is the mitigation. `memoCounter` must be pinned because *every* bundled copy runs `requestMemo()` at module scope, so every copy mints namespaces and collisions are live. Here the counter guards a construction that is itself globally deduped: `getImageRuntime()` pins `{ options, cache }` on `globalThis.__mochi_image_runtime__` in the same module that calls `new ImageCache(...)` (config.ts:117-137), synchronously, so whichever copy — server runtime or compiled island bundle — calls it first constructs the sole instance and every other copy receives that same object. Two simultaneously live `ImageCache` instances from two different copies therefore cannot arise. `ImageCache` is not exported from `index.ts` and is unreachable through the package's `exports` map (`./image` resolves to `image/index.ts`, which exports only `Image.svelte` and types; there is no wildcard subpath), so user code cannot construct a second one either. The only multi-instance sites are tests (`imageCache.test.ts:150-154`), which run in one module copy where `++cascadeSeq` is genuinely unique. Even the exotic sequence of deleting the global and re-constructing from a different copy is benign: the earlier cache is unreferenced, so replacing its handler is the desired outcome. The finding rates itself low-confidence and \"latent rather than live today\"; adopting `pinGlobal` would be harmless but fixes nothing reachable."
