    "findingId": "F5",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "Every specific claim checks out. `runSweep` emits `image:cache-sweep` only on the success path (L21) and funnels all failures into one `logger.warn` (L22-24) with no event; `cache:error`'s `operation` union is `'get' | 'set' | 'remove' | 'clear'` (events.ts:190), so no existing event covers a sweep failure, even though the cache subsystem does emit failure events elsewhere (`cache:error`, `cache:revalidate:failed`) — so this is a genuine hole rather than a subsystem-wide convention of silence. The load-bearing premise is confirmed: `ImageCache` builds its `FileStorage` with `purgeInterval: 0` (imageCache.ts:166), so this sweeper is the only janitor for the image cache, and for a `storage:` override like the documented `MemoryStorage({ maxAge })` too. Failure is reachable without any misconfiguration: `FileStorage.sweep` fans an uncapped `Promise.all` over every directory entry, `reportKeys: true` (which `ImageCache.sweep` always passes) adds a full `readKey` file read per expired entry, and pass 1's catch tolerates only ENOENT — so one EMFILE, EACCES, or (on Windows) an EPERM/EBUSY unlink against a concurrently-open file rejects the whole sweep, after partial progress. Repeated failures mean monotonic cache growth with `image:cache-sweep` telemetry simply going quiet. The re-entrancy gap is real too: `setInterval(runSweep, intervalMs)` with an async callback and no in-flight flag, which matters once `sweepIntervalMs` is set low or the directory is very large; overlap is not corrupting (duplicate unlinks mostly ENOENT) but duplicates FS load and can itself provoke the Windows unlink failures above. Two honest caveats that cap severity at BUG: `DEFAULT_LOG_LEVEL` is `'warn'` (utils/log.ts:23), so failures are visible on stderr by default and are only truly silent under `logLevel: 'error'`; and `FileStorage.startSweeper` has the identical log-only/no-guard shape. Both recommendations (emit a failure event, add `let running = false`) are correct and cheap."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F6",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "The behavior is real — `!(intervalMs > 0)` (L13) takes the no-op branch for NaN and negatives as well as the documented `0`, and `resolveImageOptions` applies `o.sweepIntervalMs ?? 3_600_000` (config.ts:106) with no finiteness check — but the finding misstates both the trigger and the inconsistency. `undefined` never reaches the guard: the `??` default catches it, and `startImageCacheSweeper`'s parameter is typed `number`, with the only production call site passing `imageRuntime.options.sweepIntervalMs` (Mochi.ts:1721). The claimed asymmetry with \"every other numeric image option\" does not hold: `Number.isFinite` appears in `image/config.ts` only inside `resolveSize` (per-size width/height/quality/rotate); none of the top-level numerics — `maxPixels`, `fetchTimeoutMs`, `maxResponseBytes`, `timeToStale`, `timeToEvict` — are validated either, so `sweepIntervalMs` is unremarkable rather than an outlier, and NaN in any of those would misbehave similarly (NaN TTLs, for instance, slip past `MochiCache`'s `minTimeToStale >= maxTimeToLive` throw because NaN comparisons are false). Triggering it requires the operator to pass an out-of-contract value: the option is typed `number` and documented as \"`0` disables it\" in both `types.ts:141` and the docs table, and nothing in the framework or any workspace in this repo derives it from an env var. The `!(x > 0)` form is the conventional NaN-safe way to express \"require a positive number\", so taking the disable branch is a deliberate fail-safe against a crash or an insane interval, not a logic error. Adding a boot-time throw or warning is a reasonable, cheap hardening suggestion — but there is no defect in the code as specified, and no in-tree path reaches it."
